# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Tool write-ups (description field) for Another Peek Beyond the Point. Keyed by tool media id."""
DESCRIPTIONS = {
"M1.S6.T6.C25.2D1": (
"Two number cards sit side by side, each showing a decimal whose point is drawn as a small dot that can be dragged. Under each card a fraction strip rewrites the number over 10, 100 or 1000, so 4.23 appears as 423/100 and 3.7 as 37/10. Below the cards are a whole-number product bar, a product card whose decimal point is still loose, and a place counter that adds up the decimal places of the two cards.\n\n"
"The student begins with a prediction: roughly how big should the product be? Then the points are removed, which turns the strips into whole numbers, and the student multiplies them; the product bar shows the digits, for example 423 times 37 = 15651. Next the student reads the two counts on the cards, 2 and 1, watches the counter add them to 3, and drags the loose point to three digits from the right of the product, which gives 15.651. When the digits run out, as in 0.432 times 0.23, the student writes zeroes in front and the product 0.09936 appears with its five places.\n\n"
"Mistakes are answered with the fraction strips. A point placed with the wrong number of places shows the strip product, for example 432/1000 times 23/100 = 9936/100000, so the five zeroes in the denominator say where the point belongs. A product with a missing front zero, such as 0.9936, is flagged as ten times too big. A size check sets the product against a rounded estimate: 4 times 4 = 16 is near 15.651, so 156.51 would be far off.\n\n"
"The six cards are 27.34 times 6 (164.04), 4.23 times 3.7 (15.651), 0.432 times 0.23 (0.09936), a card that gives 18 times 12 = 216 and asks for 0.18 times 0.12 (0.0216), 56.50 times 2.250 set beside 56.5 times 2.25 (127.125 both times, because zeroes at the end of a decimal do not change its value), and 0.306 times 1000 (the counter adds 3 and 0, and 306000 with 3 places becomes 306). Hints come in order: how many digits come after the point in each number? Then: multiply without the points, then add the two counts of decimal places. Then: count that many digits from the right of the whole-number product and place the point. Last, the strips show the product over 10, 100 or 1000 with the point placed, and a similar card follows. Through doing, the student learns that a product has as many decimal places as the two numbers together, because the denominators 10, 100 and 1000 multiply."
),

"M1.S8.T8.C34.2D1": (
"A number line from 0 to 10 runs across the top of the screen, with a magnified strip below it that zooms into 0 to 1. Two sliders set the two numbers to be multiplied, and each number is marked on the line as it moves. Under the line sit three bins labelled greater than both, between the two, and less than both, and a Check button. The first card shows 7 times 0.6.\n\n"
"The student predicts before anything is multiplied. Each multiplication card is dragged into a bin, and the student also says whether the product will be less than 1. Pressing Check drops a marker on the line at the product with both numbers marked beside it. A correct prediction is confirmed when the marker lands inside the chosen region: 7 times 0.6 = 4.2 sits between 0.6 and 7, and 0.7 times 0.6 = 0.42 falls to the left of both numbers in the zoomed strip. For 0.07 times 0.06 the strip zooms once more, to 0 to 0.1, to show 0.0042, and for 3.4 times 6.5 the line stretches to 25 so that 22.1 fits on it.\n\n"
"Then the sliders turn the cards into an experiment. The student holds one number at 5 and slides the other down from 2. At 2 the product 10 is greater than both numbers. At exactly 1 the marker lands on 5 itself. Below 1, at 0.4, the product 2 sits between 0.4 and 5. The bin label changes the moment the moving number crosses 1, so the student sees that 1 is the turning point.\n\n"
"A card in the wrong bin replays the product as a part of a number: 0.6 of 7 is less than 7, so it cannot be greater than both. A wrong claim about being less than 1 shows both numbers against 1 on the line. The six cards are 7 times 0.6 (4.2, between), 0.7 times 0.6 (0.42, less than both), 0.7 times 6 (4.2, between), 0.07 times 0.06 (0.0042, less than both), 3.4 times 6.5 (22.1, greater than both) and 0.75 times 5 (3.75, between). The two cards with 4.2 give the same product but sit between different pairs of numbers. Hints come in order: is each number greater than 1, or between 0 and 1? Then: both above 1 makes the product bigger than both, both below 1 makes it smaller than both. Then: one of each puts the product between the two numbers. Last, the product is shown on the line with both numbers, and a similar card follows. Through doing, the student learns that multiplying by a number above 1 enlarges and multiplying by a number between 0 and 1 shrinks, so the size of a product can be predicted before it is found."
),

"M2.S10.T10.C38.2D1": (
"A place value chart fills the screen, its columns running from Thousands down to Millionths, with the decimal point drawn as a fixed line between Ones and Tenths. The digits of the dividend sit on tiles in their columns, 21.1 for the first card. Four buttons across the top read divided by 10, divided by 100, divided by 1000 and divided by 10000, a tray of zero tiles waits at the side, and a small table below has a row for each number and a cell for each divisor.\n\n"
"The student first predicts the answer: will it be bigger or smaller, and how many zeroes will come straight after the point? Then a divisor button is pressed, which counts the zeroes in the divisor, and the student drags the whole row of tiles that many columns to the right. Each column a tile passes makes it one tenth as big, so the 2 Tens of 21.1 become 2 Ones and then 2 Tenths. Because the point stays still, it ends up to the left of the digits, and 21.1 divided by 10 reads 2.11. Where columns are left empty between the point and the first digit, the student drops in zero tiles, so 21.1 divided by 10000 reads 0.00211.\n\n"
"Mistakes are answered by the chart. Tiles slid the wrong way show the result as a multiplication instead, 21.1 times 10 = 211, which is not what was asked. A missing zero tile is flagged by reading the number aloud by place value: 0.13 divided by 100 written as 0.013 reads 13 thousandths, but the answer should read 13 ten-thousandths. A correct row is checked by multiplying back, when the tiles slide left by the same number of columns and land on the starting number.\n\n"
"The four rows of the table are 21.1 (2.11, 0.211, 0.0211, 0.00211), 0.13 (0.013, 0.0013, 0.00013, 0.000013), 214.6 (21.46, 2.146, 0.2146, 0.02146) and 58 (5.8, 0.58, 0.058, 0.0058), where the point of 58 sits after the Ones. The last card is a ribbon: a 3.9 m ribbon cut into 100 equal pieces gives pieces of 3.9 cm, and 3.9 cm written in metres is 3.9 divided by 100 = 0.039 m. Sliding the tiles of 0.039 back left by 2 and by 3 columns gives 3.9 cm and 39 mm. Hints ask whether the quotient is bigger or smaller than the dividend, then say that each zero in the divisor moves the point one place left, then to count the zeroes and fill the gaps with zeroes. Last, the chart shows every digit one, two or three places lower, and a similar row follows. Through doing, the student learns that dividing by 10, 100 or 1000 makes every digit one tenth, one hundredth or one thousandth as big, so the point moves left as many places as the divisor has zeroes."
),

"M2.S14.T14.C51.2D1": (
"On the left of the screen lies a tray of place value blocks, from Thousands down to Thousandths, and beside it a row of equal group boxes, as many as the divisor on the card. A Regroup button breaks one chosen block into 10 blocks of the next smaller size. Next to the tray the long division is written one line at a time as the student works. The first card is 1325 divided by 4, so the tray holds 1 Thousand, 3 Hundreds, 2 Tens and 5 Ones, and there are four boxes.\n\n"
"The student shares by hand, dragging the largest blocks into the four boxes one at a time. The 1 Thousand cannot be shared among 4, so the student presses Regroup, it becomes 10 Hundreds, and 13 Hundreds give 3 to each box with 1 left. That Hundred is regrouped into 10 Tens, and with the 2 Tens there are 12 Tens, 3 for each box. The 5 Ones give 1 to each box and 1 One is left. This is where the old method stops. Instead the student regroups the One into 10 Tenths, and at that moment the tray asks for the decimal point to be written in the quotient. The 10 Tenths give 2 each with 2 left, those regroup as 20 Hundredths and give 5 each. The tray is empty and the long division reads 331.25, which is then checked against an equivalent fraction, 1325/4 = 33125/100 = 331.25.\n\n"
"Stopping with a leftover One prompts the student to Regroup. A quotient point placed in the wrong spot is shown against the blocks in each group, where the Tenths sit to the right of the Ones. A missing zero place holder is shown as an empty Tenths column in each group, as in 0.06 divided by 5, whose quotient must be written 0.012 and not 0.12.\n\n"
"The six cards are 1325 divided by 4 (331.25), 14.4 divided by 4 (3.6, where the point goes in before the Tenths are shared), 415 divided by 4 (103.75, where the single Ten cannot be shared and the quotient needs a zero in the Tens place), 1217 divided by 2 (608.5), 4827 divided by 8 (603.375, regrouped all the way down to Thousandths) and 0.06 divided by 5 (0.012). Hints come in order: how many blocks of the largest size can each group get? Then: whatever is left is regrouped into blocks one tenth as big. Then: regroup the leftover Ones into Tenths and write the point in the quotient. Last, the tray shares the next blocks and shows the long division line, and a similar card follows. Through doing, the student learns that a remainder in the Ones is regrouped into Tenths, then Hundredths and so on, so the division carries on past the Ones until nothing is left."
),

"M2.S16.T16.C62.2D1": (
"The division appears as a fraction frame, the dividend on top and the divisor below a bar, such as 12.6 over 0.25. A button marked times 10 sits beside the frame, next to it is a lock, and a long division panel waits greyed out underneath. The student is asked to predict first whether the quotient will be bigger or smaller than 12.6.\n\n"
"Pressing times 10 multiplies the top and the bottom together. The frame redraws as 126 over 2.5, with an equals sign to the old fraction, so the student sees that its value has not changed. The divisor is still not whole, so the student presses again and the frame reads 1260 over 25. Now the divisor is a counting number, the long division panel opens, and the student divides 1260 by 25 by the place value method, regrouping the 10 left over in the Ones into Tenths, to get 50.4. If the student tries to multiply only the divisor, the lock refuses and reminds that the fraction would change.\n\n"
"Every answer is checked by multiplying the quotient by the original divisor: 50.4 times 0.25 gives back 12.6. A quotient that fails the check is shown against the original division, so the student can see which step slipped. Some cards need only one press, since 46.8 divided by 1.3 becomes 468 over 13 and gives 36, and 1.872 divided by 0.1 becomes 18.72 over 1, which shows that dividing by 0.1 is the same as multiplying by 10. Two cards need no press at all, 2 divided by 5 (0.4) and 13 divided by 4 (3.25), because their divisors are whole already; the tool says so and goes straight to the long division past the Ones. The sixth card, 12.43 divided by 0.6, clears to 124.3 over 6 and gives 20.71666..., where the remainder 4 keeps returning. The tool writes the dots and notes that this division does not stop, so its check lands very close to 12.43 (20.7166 times 0.6 = 12.42996) and not exactly on it.\n\n"
"Hints come in order: is the divisor a whole number yet? Then: multiply the dividend and the divisor by the same 10 or 100. Then: use the power of 10 that clears every decimal place in the divisor, then divide. Last, the cleared division and its long division are shown, and a similar card follows. Through doing, the student learns that multiplying the dividend and the divisor by the same 10, 100 or 1000 leaves the quotient unchanged, so a decimal divisor can always be turned into a whole number before dividing."
),

"M2.S18.T18.C72.2D1": (
"A long division waits on the left of the screen, ready to run one step per tap, and beside it stands a ring of numbered slots, 0 up to one less than the divisor. For the first card, 10 divided by 3, the ring has the three slots 0, 1 and 2. A box above the division asks whether this division will end or go on for ever.\n\n"
"The student predicts first, then taps. Each tap runs one step: the tool writes the next amount to share, for example 10 Tenths, and the student may type the quotient digit before it is shown. A digit that is too small leaves a remainder at least as big as the divisor, and this is flagged as a missed quotient digit. Each new remainder lights its slot in the ring. For 10 divided by 3 the first step gives 3 with remainder 1, slot 1 lights, and the very next step gives 3 with remainder 1 again. The ring flashes, the repeating digit 3 is underlined, and the student writes 3.333... and names the block that repeats. For 1 divided by 7 the remainders light the ring one by one, 1, 3, 2, 6, 4, 5, and then 1 comes back. Six of the seven slots are lit, slot 0 never lights because a remainder 0 would end the division, and the digits 142857 repeat in the same order.\n\n"
"Claims are tested. If the student says a division ends, the tool runs on to the first repeat or to remainder 0, and the first repeated remainder is highlighted together with the step where it first appeared, so the student sees the cycle close. The six cards are 10 divided by 3 (3.333..., remainder 1 repeats), 10 divided by 9 (1.111..., only slot 1 lights), 100 divided by 11 (9.0909..., the remainders 1 and 10 take turns and the block is 09), 1 divided by 7 (0.142857 repeating), 1 divided by 17 (a ring of 17 slots, sixteen remainders and a block of sixteen digits, 0588235294117647) and 13 divided by 4 (3.25, which ends at remainder 0 after the remainders 1, 2, 0).\n\n"
"Hints come in order: what remainders are possible when dividing by this number? Then: if a remainder comes back, the same steps follow again. Then: keep dividing and list the remainders until one repeats or one is 0. Last, the remainder ring shows the repeat and the repeating digits, and a similar card follows. Through doing, the student learns that every remainder is less than the divisor, so a division by a whole number either reaches 0 and ends or returns to a remainder it has had and repeats for ever."
),

"M2.S20.T20.C80.2D1": (
"One long bar stands for the dividend, 128 on the first card, and beneath it a second bar shows the quotient, redrawn every time the divisor changes. A slider sets the divisor from 0.1 to 10 with a notch at 1, and a small zoom lets it go below 0.1 when a card needs it. Three bins wait at the bottom: quotient bigger than the dividend, equal, and smaller.\n\n"
"The student bins each card before dividing, for example 128 divided by 4 or 128 divided by 0.4. Pressing Check prints the quotient, 32 or 320, and the quotient bar settles beside the dividend bar. The bar shows the division as how many pieces of the divisor fit into the dividend. When the quotient is very much longer, as 800 is against 2.4, the dividend bar is shown as a short marked piece at the start of the quotient bar. After Check the student explains the bin by choosing the sentence that says whether the divisor is below or above 1 and so whether more or fewer pieces than the dividend's worth fit inside it.\n\n"
"The slider turns the cards into an experiment. With the dividend set to 12.5, the student drags the divisor down from 5. At 5 the quotient bar is 2.5, at 2.5 it is 5, and at 1 the two bars have exactly the same length. Below 1 the quotient bar grows past the dividend bar, and at 0.5 it is twice as long, 25. The tool points out the moment the quotient bar crosses the dividend bar, as the divisor passes 1.\n\n"
"A wrong bin replays the division as how many divisors fit inside the dividend, and a correct bin is confirmed with the quotient value. The six cards are 128 divided by 4 (32, smaller), 128 divided by 0.4 (320, bigger), 2.4 divided by 0.003 (800, bigger), 1.872 divided by 0.1 (18.72, bigger), 15.6 divided by 0.1 (156, bigger) and 12.5 divided by 2.5 (5, smaller). Hints come in order: is the divisor greater than 1 or less than 1? Then: count how many of the divisor fit into the dividend. Then: a divisor below 1 fits more times than the dividend itself. Last, the bars show the quotient against the dividend, and a similar card follows. Through doing, the student learns that dividing by a number greater than 1 gives a quotient smaller than the dividend, and dividing by a number between 0 and 1 gives a bigger one."
),

"M3.S22.T22.C90.2D1": (
"A ledger page opens for a chosen stretch of years, with buttons for 4, 100, 400, 1000 and 10000 years. Down the left are three rule switches: a leap day every 4th year, skip the leap day in every 100th year, and keep it in every 400th year. The page counts the 366-day leap years and the 365-day ordinary years, writes the calendar total of days beside the true total (365.2422 times the number of years), and shows the gap in days as a bar. A strip of years below lights each leap year.\n\n"
"The student first predicts whether the calendar will run ahead of the Earth or behind it, and types how many leap years the switched-on rules will give. With every switch off, 100 years of 365 days make 36500 days against 36524.22, so the calendar is 24.22 days behind and the gap bar is red. Switching on the 4th year rule gives 25 leap and 75 ordinary years, 36525 days against 36524.22, and the calendar is now 0.78 days ahead, an overcompensation. On the 4 year span the ledger reads 1461 against 1460.9688. Switching on the skip rule gives 24 leap and 76 ordinary years, 36524 days, and the calendar is 0.22 days behind. On 1000 years the skip rule alone leaves 365240 against 365242.2, short by 2.2 days, and the bar turns red. Then the 400 rule gives 242 leap and 758 ordinary years, 365242 days, only 0.2 days behind.\n\n"
"A wrong count of leap years is checked year by year on the strip. The gap bar turns red whenever the calendar is more than a day off. The six cards are 4 years with every 4th year leap (1461 against 1460.9688), 100 years with every 4th year leap (36525 against 36524.22), 100 years with the 100th year skipped (36524 against 36524.22), 1000 years with the 400 rule (365242 against 365242.2), 10000 years with the 400 rule (2425 leap years, 3652425 against 3652422, three days ahead and red) and a fix of the student's own for 10000 years. For the fix the student types a number N, and the ledger removes the leap day from every leap year that N divides. With N = 4000 two leap days go, from the years 4000 and 8000, and the gap falls from 3 days to 1 day. If N picks out years that were not leap years anyway, the ledger says that no leap day was removed and the gap stays.\n\n"
"Hints come in order: how many days does the Earth really take for these years? Then: multiply 365.2422 by the number of years and compare with the calendar. Then: count the leap years under the rules switched on, then add one day for each to 365 times the years. Last, the ledger shows both totals and the gap, and a similar span follows. Through doing, the student learns that a calendar rule is judged by comparing its days over many years with 365.2422 times the years, and that each new rule fixes the gap left by the one before it."
),
}
